WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:15.600
On the cellphones, in the streets, in the
cars: the beats of a rap song reverberate

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.490
throughout Thailand.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:27.019
Prathet Gu Mi, or in English: "What my country
has", criticizes Thailand’s military government

00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:36.920
and the lack of freedom.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:42.079
In a country under a military dictatorship
THAT has struck a nerve, especially among

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:43.370
young Thais.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:47.920
The song got more than 20 million clicks on
youtube in just a week.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:54.140
It’s sort of a voice for teenagers, for
young adults, who can think but can’t act.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:58.600
We can only talk amongst each other but we
have no liberty or rights to speak out freely.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:04.570
If you do speak your mind it’ll only be
bad for yourself.

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:12.740
And you can’t change anything anyway.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:18.070
Not daring to speak out – something the
group Rappers against Dictatorship wanted

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:19.070
to change.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:22.520
That’s why they wrote Prathet Gu Mi.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.090
We think that anyone can use the phrase “what
my country’s got...”, the title of the

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.399
song to speak out about whatever’s on their
mind, what they think is the matter with our

00:01:30.399 --> 00:01:31.689
country.

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:40.210
We hope people will start being more critical
when it comes to politics.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:45.439
Speaking out against the powerful has never
been without risk in Thailand.

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:51.979
But since the latest military coup four years
ago, it’s become even more dangerous.

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:55.039
Also for Rappers Against Dictatorship.

00:01:55.039 --> 00:01:59.290
The police at first declared they were investigating
the group for a possible violation of the

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:03.990
so called ‘computer crime act’ - it could
get them 5 years in prison.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:09.479
But what that announcement got the SONG, was
5 million CLICKS in two days.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:13.030
The authorities have since backed down.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.350
The lyrics don’t contain anything that insults
anyone personally.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:20.290
That’s why I never thought the song would
draw attention from the authorities.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:25.200
But when the police started talking about
it, that’s really when it became so popular

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:33.640
all of a sudden.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:42.130
On the outside, Thailand looks like a peaceful
country – but there are deep divisions.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:47.780
Time and again, the country has been on the
brink of civil war.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:53.670
For Now, the military government is keeping
a lid on things, but below the surface its

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.800
starting to heat up again.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.650
There haven’t been elections in more than
four years.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.379
They’re scheduled for February.

00:03:02.379 --> 00:03:07.730
But what will they be worth if society doesn’t
change?

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:10.840
People should develop a critical mindset in
their everyday lives.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:17.190
I think if that were the case, there would
actually be less conflict within society.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:20.450
Conflict happens when people don’t accept
that others have a different opinion.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:25.980
If we could get there, reach a compromise
instead of fighting with those who don’t

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:56.750
share our opinion, that would be an important
step toward a better future.

